WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.350
Everyone will remember Stan Lee as the lovable
grandpa of the Marvel universe...

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.150
"Are you Tony Stank?"

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:11.670
All those wonderful cameos aside, Stan Lee
was a cornerstone of the comics industry dating

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.290
all the way back to 1939.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:18.409
For eight decades, Lee compiled a remarkable
list of achievements and changed comics forever

00:00:18.409 --> 00:00:22.990
— but there's still plenty that many Marvel
fans don't know about his life and legacy.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.359
Let's take some time to remember and appreciate
Stan Lee for introducing us to Earth's mightiest

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.360
heroes.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:33.420
Stan Lee was born Stanley Martin Lieber in
1922, which was perfect timing to enter the

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.690
workforce during the Great Depression.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:40.370
He was always interested in writing, and when
he turned 18, he got a job with Timely Comics,

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.490
the company that would later become Marvel.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:46.410
It just happened to be owned by Martin Goodman,
who was married to Lee’s cousin.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.510
How much this family connection helped Lee
get the job remains disputed.

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.630
At the time, Lee assumed his career in comics
would be short-lived, and worried that it

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.580
might hurt his ambition to become a respected
novelist.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.850
As a result, he adopted "Stan Lee" as a pen
name and spent years introducing himself to

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:03.900
people simply as "a writer," without telling
them what he wrote.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.670
Lee had been working at Marvel for less than
a year when Joe Simon and Jack Kirby discovered

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.749
that Martin Goodman had been stiffing them
out of royalties on their popular creation,

00:01:12.749 --> 00:01:13.749
Captain America.

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:17.750
Outraged, Kirby and Simon quit Timely for
National Comics, the company that would later

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:18.960
become DC.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.270
Goodman immediately appointed the 19-year-old
Stan Lee to replace Simon as editor-in-chief.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.549
At the time, he had mostly written short "filler"
comics and brief text stories that were only

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:31.869
included so the comics could get a cheaper
rate on postage.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:35.990
According to Lee, the appointment was supposed
to be temporary, but Goodman became distracted

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.719
with other branches of his publishing company
and forgot to replace him.

00:01:39.719 --> 00:01:44.079
As it turned out, Lee remained in the post
for the next three decades, although, like

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:48.439
most comics creators of his generation, he
did take a few years out to serve in the army

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:50.130
during World War II.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.570
It was there that he had his greatest fame
as an artist, designing posters warning soldiers

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:59.039
against sexually transmitted diseases, with
the catchy caption "VD?

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.040
Not Me!"

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.759
After the war, superheroes suffered a shockingly
rapid decline in popularity, and over at Timely,

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:07.799
all superhero comics were canceled.

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:11.720
Lee, desperate to stay in business, had little
room for creativity.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.670
Despite efforts to copy other companies’
success with Westerns and monster comics,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:20.470
the company repeatedly risked bankruptcy during
the 1950s, to the point that Jack Kirby said

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.130
he once came in to find Lee sobbing while
movers were taking furniture out of the office.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:30.220
Goodman eventually made Lee fire all the other
employees, including many of his closest friends,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:31.760
but kept Lee himself around.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.330
When Lee was 39, he told his wife, Joan, that
he wanted to quit, but DC had recently reinvigorated

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.330
superhero comics with their new team book,
the Justice League of America.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.930
In an attempt to replicate those sales, Lee
and Jack Kirby co-created the Fantastic Four,

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.590
a huge hit that saved the company and changed
comics history.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.590
Steve Ditko's departure

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.770
With Steve Ditko, Stan had co-created Spider-Man
and Doctor Strange, but by the mid-60s, their

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.030
relationship had deteriorated to the point
of being almost nonexistent.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.360
Ditko had always been a little intense, but
as his tenure on Amazing Spider-Man continued,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.130
he attempted to inject the stories with references
to Ayn Rand's Objectivist philosophy, in which

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:09.980
he was an ardent believer.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.400
That put him at odds with Lee, and eventually,
they just stopped speaking to each other,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.750
even as they continued working on the comic.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.190
The final straw came when the two creators
disagreed over the true identity of the Green

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.190
Goblin.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.580
Lee wanted it to be Norman Osborn, the father
of Spider-Man's best friend; Ditko allegedly

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.060
wanted the Goblin to be an unrelated criminal.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.190
Lee won the argument.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.320
Ditko exited the book and was replaced by
John Romita Sr., who stayed with Marvel many

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.690
years.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.220
Despite their disputes, Lee and Ditko were
two forces to be reckoned with — and both

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.160
left us in the same year.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.500
Ditko passed away on June 29th of a heart
attack at the age of 90, while Lee followed

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.100
a few months later on November 12th at the
age of 95.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.920
On the page, Stan Lee and Jack Kirby may have
been the team responsible for building the

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.810
majority of the Marvel Universe and telling
some of the best comic book stories of all

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:00.050
time, but behind the scenes, Kirby’s frustration
with his collaborator was growing throughout

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.130
the '60s.

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:05.291
To Kirby, Stan was the consummate corporate
creator, serving as the editor-in-chief and

00:04:05.291 --> 00:04:08.880
the public face of the company that Kirby
saw as exploiting his talent.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.840
When he started at DC, Kirby wasted no time
in making his feelings known by turning his

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.430
collaborator-turned-nemesis into a comics
character.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:22.060
In the pages of Mister Miracle #6, Kirby introduced
Funky Flashman, telling readers “I know

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.280
him, you know him, but does anybody need him?"

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.960
Accompanied by a sniveling minion named Houseroy,
thought to be based on Stan’s assistant,

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:34.750
Roy Thomas, Flashman was a con man out to
exploit others’ talent, and was first seen

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:39.010
taking an allowance that was automatically
dispensed from a statue, literally pulling

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.820
the money out of someone else’s head

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.270
Despite the bad blood, Kirby and Lee were
able to make peace in 1993, shortly before

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.270
Kirby’s passing.

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:51.260
"I don't think there's any artist in the field
today who doesn't owe Jack something."

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.100
In the 1960s, Marvel was just a small division
of a company called Magazine Management, and

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.740
they weren't actually the most profitable
part of the company.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:03.510
That honor went to a line of "Men's
Magazines" with extremely creative names like

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:06.320
Stag, Male, and Complete Man.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.410
Sensing an opportunity to capitalize on that
success, Lee decided to combine Magazine Management's

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:13.550
two most prominent departments.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:19.020
The result was The Adventures of Pussycat,
which made its debut in 1965 as what Lee called

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.740
a “cutie” comic.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.560
"Oh man…"

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.760
While Lee's gamble on The Adventures of Pussycat
didn't really pay off, the scandalous story

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.350
included work from Lee, Wally Wood, and Al
Hartley, who wound up leaving Pussycat and

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.120
Marvel after finding religion.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.390
He spent his remaining career drawing Christian-themed
Archie comics.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:42.870
It's easy to argue that Stan's most important
role wasn't as a comic book writer or editor

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.040
but as an ambassador for the medium.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.430
Long before he was a notable cameo in the
world's most popular movies, he served his

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:53.410
ambassador role with a set of videos designed
to give viewers a look behind the scenes of

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:54.920
the comic book industry.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:00.490
Released on VHS in 1991, The Comic Book Greats
was a 12-part documentary series hosted by

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.340
Lee where he interviewed creators about their
process.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:08.169
Not only did it feature legends like Batman
co-creator Bob Kane, it also put the spotlight

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:10.360
on some of the hottest creators of the era.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:15.040
Among the most interesting segments are Stan's
interviews with Jim Lee, Todd McFarlane, Rob

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:19.460
Liefeld, and Whilce Portacio, four of the
artists whose incredible success at Marvel

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.610
would lead them to found Image Comics.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.820
McFarlane and Liefeld's interviews even managed
to feature obscure first appearances of a

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.340
characters that would later make their way
into the comics.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.639
Challenged by Stan to create a whole new hero
or villain on a tight time limit, Liefeld

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:36.610
drew Die Hard, a future member of his super-team,
Youngblood — and quickly had a cheerful

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:39.580
Stan asking questions about what he called
our new character.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:45.389
Stan always had a knack for following trends,
so it's no surprise that in 1998, he co-founded

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:49.909
a company called Stan Lee Media in order to
produce "webisodes" for an Internet audience

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:53.120
— including an animated comic about the
Backstreet Boys.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:54.620
"No way!

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:56.220
I hate when that happens."

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:00.990
It also shouldn't be surprising that in 2000,
when the dotcom bubble burst, SLM went defunct

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:02.680
and filed for bankruptcy.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.669
What happened after that, however, nobody
saw coming.

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:09.880
The short version is that SLM claimed that
Lee assigned all of the rights to his characters

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:14.520
to the company, and they've been suing Marvel,
Disney, and Lee himself for billions of dollars

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.520
since 2007.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.280
"Do what I ask or Earth and all your friends
are doomed."

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.310
It probably goes without saying, but the lawsuits
and appeals have consistently been called

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.970
"frivolous" and dismissed in court.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:30.280
Lee himself did his best to extract himself
from both the company and its ongoing legal

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.280
mess.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.690
Unfortunately, the one thing that isn't in
dispute is that they own the name "Stan Lee

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:38.900
Media," linking Lee's name to their actions
despite his best efforts to shut them down.

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.940
"Ah, forget it."

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:45.320
The ups and downs of Stan Lee's career aside,
he co-created and helped introduce some of

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:47.510
our favorite characters to the world.

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:51.220
With the help of late, great legends like
Jack Kirby and Steve Ditko, Stan Lee made

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:56.370
the Fantastic Four, Spider-Man, the X-Men,
Iron Man, Thor, Black Panther, the Hulk, and

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.270
many other iconic characters household names.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:03.050
As the Marvel Cinematic Universe is starting
to eclipse its printed counterparts in terms

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:06.960
of pop culture influence and recognition,
we should remember Stan Lee for being the

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.220
wonderful grandfather of superherodom that
he was, whether it was being the ambassador

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.040
to the comic book industry or giving Peter
Parker amazing advice on-screen.

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:22.880
"I guess one person can make a difference…
enough said."

